✅ What is OOP?
OOP is a programming paradigm based on objects and classes.
Helps in code reusability, modularity, and maintainability.

✅ Class
Blueprint for creating objects.
Contains fields (data) and methods (behavior).
Example: class Car { String color; void drive(){} }

✅ Object
Instance of a class.
Created using new keyword.
Example: Car c = new Car();

✅ Main Principles
Encapsulation
Wrapping data and methods together.
Achieved using private fields and getter/setter.
Example: private int age; public void setAge(int a){ age=a; }

Inheritance
One class acquires properties of another.
Achieved using extends keyword.
Example: class Dog extends Animal { }

Polymorphism
Compile-time: Method overloading.
Runtime: Method overriding.
Example: void show(int a); void show(String b);
Same method behaves differently based on context.
Types: Compile-time (method overloading) and Runtime (method overriding).

Abstraction
Hiding implementation details, showing only functionality.
Achieved using abstract classes and javainterfaces.

✅ Other Concepts
Class → Blueprint for objects.
Object → Instance of a class.
Constructor → Initializes object.
Access Modifiers → public, private, protected, default.
1. public:
   ✔ Accessible from anywhere
   → Same class
   → Same package
   → Different package
   → Through child class
   → Through object
2. private:
   ✔ Accessible only inside the same class
   ❌ Not accessible from: other classes, subclasses, other packages
3. default (no keyword):
   ✔ Accessible within the same package only
   ❌ Not accessible from: classes in other packages (even subclasses)
4. protected:
   ✔ Accessible in: Same class, Same package, Subclass (even in different package)
   ❌ Not accessible in non-subclass classes of another package.

Interfaces
Contains abstract methods only (Java 8+ allows default methods).
Implemented using implements keyword.
Example: interface Drawable { void draw(); }
class-->implements interface
interface->extends interface

Abstract Class
An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated (you cannot create objects of it).
It may contain abstract methods (without body) and concrete methods (with body).
Declared with abstract keyword.
Cannot be instantiated.
Example: abstract class Shape { abstract void draw(); }

Default Class (Package-Private Class)
There is no keyword called "default class" in Java.
But when you don’t write any access modifier for a class, it is called a default (package-private) class.
→ It is accessible only within the same package.

Static Class
Java does not allow top-level classes to be static.
However, nested classes (inner classes) can be static.

Final Class
A final class cannot be inherited.

Concrete Class
A normal class (non-abstract) that provides complete implementation.
